{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 10a5a13431179420a275453892cbb163a97814e6, judging artifacts 7acc4e33f76ed29dc3757601557c58a9e11b6777 for docs/reference/architecture.md, cbf97e8fd57ebf79913d2eb81eb8f1f4491bc0e3 for AGENTS.md, and 6c45c56dc589233b6d06dfd262d2a61b81f2daa9 for docs/README.md.\n\nI checked the code obeys the contract rather than that the prose reads well, because a contract page the code contradicts is worse than none: it teaches a reader something false about where responsibility lives.\n\nARCHITECTURE AND CONTRACT. Affected layers are documentation and repository policy only; no Go source changes, so no layer's runtime contract changes. The head is 268 insertions and no deletions across AGENTS.md, docs/README.md, and a new docs/reference/architecture.md. Every dependency claim the page makes holds at this commit, verified by go list -deps rather than by reading imports: internal/intent and internal/kernel each reach zero internal/workroom packages; internal/workroom reaches no gitstore, service, nexus, net/http or MCP package; internal/gitstore reaches no workroom, statusview or app; internal/nexus reaches no workroom; internal/app reaches both kernel and workroom, which is the join the page claims for it. Every one of the fourteen packages and commands the page names exists at this head. workroom.ProfileVersion exists, at internal/workroom/schema.go:17, and is \"workroom-fold@2\". The refs/seq claim is consistent with internal/gitstore/graph.go:24, which excludes refs/seq/* from ordinary ref walking for the stated reason.\n\nSECURITY BOUNDARIES. The load-bearing claim is at line 93: the application admission hook cannot inspect application payload bytes, so it cannot silently turn the kernel into an interpreter. That is structurally true rather than merely asserted. internal/kernel/kernel.go:114 defines Admission as Intent, ActorKey, CapabilityProof; internal/intent/intent.go:50 shows Intent carrying PayloadTree, a hash string, and no payload bytes. The kernel's own Event type does carry Payload []byte, and Admission deliberately does not. The hook therefore sees envelope facts and capability material only, exactly as documented. No new attack surface: the head adds no code and no dependency. The AGENTS.md addition strengthens the boundary by requiring a security conclusion from every future review.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. None that I would ask for. I considered three. The layered narrative in sections one to six and the package table near the end describe most packages twice, but they serve different reading paths — sequential understanding and lookup — and collapsing them would cost the reader one of those. The five-axis compatibility table is substantive rather than ceremonial: each axis has a distinct marker and the table is what makes \"compatible with Gitseq\" answerable. The AGENTS.md addition is three bullets and could not be shorter without losing the requirement that each conclusion be recorded. I found no avoidable machinery.\n\nPOLICY PLACEMENT. Confirmed. The repository-specific pre-merge policy is in AGENTS.md, added inside step 4 where a reviewer meets it before approving. SKILL.md is unchanged at this head; git diff --name-only against main returns no match for it. The division the page states — AGENTS.md governs this repository, SKILL.md is reusable Workroom guidance — is honoured by the change itself.\n\nGATES. go test ./internal/docset passes at this head, 316s, which is the gate that actually exercises documentation. The worktree is clean at the exact head.\n\nONE NON-BLOCKING FINDING, and I want to be explicit that I am not asking this head to fix it. The page's frontmatter rests on two artifacts. One, at internal/kernel/kernel.go, is a real path and can be superseded, so the page will flare when the kernel moves. The other is a six-way comma-joined pseudo-path. Paths match as exact strings, so no merge will ever publish a successor equal to that string, and the page cannot flare on changes to app, workroom, service, intent or nexus — five of the six areas that basis nominally covers. That is a real weakness in a page whose own Review rule depends on flaring.\n\nI nearly made it blocking and I was wrong to consider it. Twenty of the forty-six anchored documentation pages already rest on a comma path; docs/reference/gs/batch.md has no other basis at all. This is the prevailing convention, not something this head introduced, and refusing the house style one page at a time would be singling out the head that happened to arrive while I was looking. AGENTS.md already says the comma-path artifacts take their own repair under their own request, and none existed, so I filed it as f296743a327b01b111528d904ca61eaf23845030 rather than charging it to you. Worth noting in your favour: the three candidate artifacts for this head are each at a single real path, which is the correct pattern.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:7acc4e33f76ed29dc3757601557c58a9e11b6777","head":"10a5a13431179420a275453892cbb163a97814e6","verdict":"approved"}}